---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import PdfSecurePasswordProtect from '@/components/tools/pdf/PdfSecurePasswordProtect.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="password-protect-pdf">
  <PdfSecurePasswordProtect client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <section>
      <h2>How to password protect a PDF</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Drop the PDF onto the box above. It is read straight into this tab's memory.</li>
        <li>
          Type the password people will need to open it, then type it again. The strength meter is
          only advice. Length matters much more than punctuation.
        </li>
        <li>
          Optionally open <strong>permissions and owner password</strong> to withhold printing,
          copying or editing from anyone who does not also hold the owner password.
        </li>
        <li>
          Press <strong>Encrypt with AES-256</strong>. Progress is reported per object; a large
          document contains tens of thousands of them.
        </li>
        <li>
          Check the self-test result, then download. Store the password somewhere you will still
          have it in a year.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What is actually being written into the file</h2>
      <p>
        The PDF format itself defines encryption. To password protect a PDF, this tool writes the
        standard security handler at <strong>version 5, revision 6</strong>: the scheme from
        ISO 32000-2 and the strongest that the specification defines.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          A random 256-bit <strong>file encryption key</strong> is generated by your browser's
          CSPRNG. It is not derived from your password; your password unlocks it.
        </li>
        <li>
          Your password is run through <em>Algorithm 2.B</em>: at least sixty-four rounds of
          SHA-256, SHA-384 or SHA-512 interleaved with AES-128-CBC, with the choice of hash at each
          round decided by the previous round's output. This is what makes guessing slow.
        </li>
        <li>
          That derived key encrypts the file key, and the result is stored as <code>/UE</code>
          (and <code>/OE</code> for the owner password). The file key itself is never stored in the
          clear anywhere.
        </li>
        <li>
          Then each string and stream in the document (page content, embedded fonts, images,
          bookmarks, metadata) is encrypted with AES-256-CBC under the file key, each with its own
          random initialisation vector.
        </li>
        <li>
          The permission flags are packed into a 16-byte block, encrypted with the file key and
          written as <code>/Perms</code>, so a reader can detect that they have been tampered with.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The whole object graph is rewritten, so the tool serialises the document itself and does not
        use the library's ordinary save path. A normal save would add new, unencrypted objects
        afterwards and give a file that PDF readers reject.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Open password vs owner password</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Password</th>
              <th scope="col">Also called</th>
              <th scope="col">What it does</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Open password</td>
              <td>User password, document open password</td>
              <td>
                Required before the document can be decrypted at all. Without it the file is
                unreadable ciphertext.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Owner password</td>
              <td>Permissions password, master password</td>
              <td>
                Lifts the restrictions below. A reader given only the open password sees the
                document but is held to the permission flags.
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        If you leave the owner password toggle off, both are set to the same value, which is the
        right choice for the common case of "nobody opens this without the password". Use two
        different passwords when you want to hand a document out for reading but keep printing or
        copying for yourself.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>How strong does the password need to be?</h2>
      <p>
        Revision 6's key derivation is expensive on purpose, but it is not a modern memory-hard
        function like Argon2. An attacker with GPUs can still test many candidates per second. So
        length is what matters.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Four random dictionary words</strong> (about 44 bits) is fine for stopping a
          colleague or a casual finder.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Five or six random words</strong>, or sixteen-plus random characters, is the right
          level for anything with a legal or financial consequence.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Anything reused</strong> from another account is the weakest option regardless of
          how complicated it looks, because the attacker starts from a leaked list, not from
          scratch.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Our <a href="/password-generator">password generator</a> and
        <a href="/passphrase-generator">passphrase generator</a> both run in the browser too, so you
        can create the password without it touching a network either.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What encryption does not do</h2>
      <p>Being straightforward about the limits is more useful than a security badge:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Permission flags are a request, not enforcement. Mainstream PDF readers
          respect them. A command-line tool holding the correct password does not have to. If the
          content must not be printed by a determined recipient, encryption is the wrong control.
        </li>
        <li>
          Screenshots exist. Anyone who can read a document can photograph it.
        </li>
        <li>
          It does not prove who wrote the document. That is a digital signature,
          which needs a certificate authority and is a different mechanism entirely.
        </li>
        <li>
          Existing digital signatures will break. Re-encrypting rewrites every
          object, which invalidates any signature already applied. Sign after encrypting, not
          before.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Why doing this locally matters here more than anywhere</h2>
      <p>
        An upload-based "protect PDF" service needs two things at once: the confidential document and
        its password. Both are then together on a machine you do not control. News stories about
        document-conversion sites that left millions of uploaded files public show that risk.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here your browser reads the document, your operating system's random number generator makes
        the key, and the same tab builds the encrypted result. To verify, open your browser's
        developer tools, switch to the Network panel, and encrypt a file. Or turn off Wi-Fi first: the
        page still works.
      </p>
    </section>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
